WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Governance controls help teams avoid accidental waste without stopping useful engineering.

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.400
Budgets make expected spend visible before it becomes a surprise.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:14.000
Alerts can warn owners when a project, environment, or workload is trending beyond expectations.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.400
Tags are the language of ownership.

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.800
A useful tagging plan connects resources to teams, projects, environments, cost centers, owners, and lifecycle state.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.200
Without tags, cost review becomes guesswork.

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.000
With tags, teams can connect spend to purpose and ask better questions.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.800
Quotas and default limits prevent accidental scale from becoming open-ended spend.

9
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:42.000
They should be high enough to support legitimate work and low enough to force review before expensive growth.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.200
Exception handling should be clear so urgent business needs have a responsible path.

11
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.000
Approval workflows should match risk and value.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.800
A small experiment may need lightweight controls.

13
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.600
A production inference deployment or large training run may need stronger review.

14
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:04.000
Guardrails are most effective when they block risky defaults, require ownership, and make cost impact visible.

15
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.400
Engineers need feedback.

16
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.400
If the people creating workloads cannot see spend, utilization, and ownership, they cannot improve behavior.

17
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.800
Cost control works best when financial signals appear close to the technical choices that create them.

18
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.600
Guardrails should also be humane and practical.

19
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:29.200
A blocked deployment should explain what rule failed, who can approve an exception, and what safer default is expected.

20
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.400
Clear feedback turns policy into guidance instead of friction for busy engineering teams.
